We're taking a three week break.

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:54.533
Am I right, Kiko?

00:18:54.533 --> 00:18:56.323
Three weeks without Ear Biscuits.

00:18:57.250 --> 00:18:59.310
Find another podcast, no
don't find another podcast.

00:18:59.310 --> 00:19:02.500
Listen to old Ear Biscuits,
do that in the three weeks

00:19:02.500 --> 00:19:03.333
that we're not here.

00:19:03.333 --> 00:19:04.870
Don't get hooked on anybody else now.

00:19:04.870 --> 00:19:07.600
And then we'll be back
with more, so next to last,

00:19:07.600 --> 00:19:09.440
we've got one more next week
and then a three week break

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:10.340
and we'll be back.

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:15.843
I'll quickly go through
the rhythm of tour.

00:19:16.780 --> 00:19:18.410
Because I think it can be romanticized

00:19:18.410 --> 00:19:19.780
and there's many ways to do it,

00:19:19.780 --> 00:19:23.300
I mean we're spoiled
on a number of fronts.

00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:25.720
First of all I just wanna
brag about everybody

00:19:25.720 --> 00:19:29.873
who came out to this leg
of the tour and saw us.

00:19:31.590 --> 00:19:36.590
You guys were super
supportive and welcoming,

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:42.210
and even, I'm always fascinated
and we're so analytical

00:19:42.641 --> 00:19:46.620
when it comes to anything we
create or any time we perform,

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:48.760
like the moment we're
done putting something out

00:19:48.760 --> 00:19:51.860
into the world or we come
backstage after something,

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:55.366
we're immediately
assessing how well it went.

00:19:55.366 --> 00:19:59.840
And it never fails to surprise me how

00:19:59.840 --> 00:20:01.710
different audiences are different.

00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:04.750
Well how all audiences are
a little bit different,

00:20:04.750 --> 00:20:08.250
the things that as a whole
that resonate with them.

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:10.790
Some audiences are really,
they like to cheer,

00:20:10.790 --> 00:20:14.030
some audiences like to laugh,
some audiences do both.

00:20:14.030 --> 00:20:15.430
Some like to talk.

00:20:15.430 --> 00:20:19.547
Some like to talk to us and I think we,

00:20:19.547 --> 00:20:22.160
there are cues that we
give that welcome that

00:20:22.160 --> 00:20:24.518
and sometimes at the start of a show,

00:20:24.518 --> 00:20:27.950
we kinda, we're a little
more open than other times.

00:20:27.950 --> 00:20:32.491
And we made it a point
to try a few new songs

00:20:32.491 --> 00:20:36.520
and the banter in
between, that's our moment

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:38.680
to see if we can create
something new every single time,

00:20:38.680 --> 00:20:39.513
give or take.

00:20:42.032 --> 00:20:47.032
We talked about how when
comedians or musicians tour,

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:48.790
a lot of times they have
to win over the audience

00:20:48.790 --> 00:20:51.110
and we never have to do that
because people who show up

00:20:51.110 --> 00:20:54.581
are dedicated Mythical Beasts
so there's this comfort level

00:20:54.581 --> 00:20:57.970
and so like I said, we're
spoiled on that front.

00:20:57.970 --> 00:21:00.990
We also have a bus that we travel in.

00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:04.650
Now when the bus goes
a really long distance

00:21:04.650 --> 00:21:09.650
in between shows, we do fly but
we finally realized it took,

00:21:10.230 --> 00:21:11.920
for most of the Tour of
Mythicality we didn't do this

00:21:11.920 --> 00:21:15.197
but we started to realize
towards the end that hey,

00:21:15.197 --> 00:21:17.870
we can sleep on the bus,
this can actually be fun.

00:21:17.870 --> 00:21:20.480
This might be the best
part of the whole thing.

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:22.220
Well maybe not.

00:21:22.220 --> 00:21:25.090
For a 6'7" young man.

00:21:25.090 --> 00:21:27.700
Well considering that the bunks are 6'6".

00:21:27.700 --> 00:21:28.960
Yeah.

00:21:28.960 --> 00:21:30.460
In length.

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:33.537
But we are spoiled to have a
bus that has sleeping bunks.

00:21:33.537 --> 00:21:34.790
A nice bus.
And then it pulls

00:21:34.790 --> 00:21:37.160
a trailer with merch and stuff behind it

00:21:37.160 --> 00:21:40.270
and so us and the crew,
we basically traveled

00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:43.700
on a bus the whole time, I
also brought my kids Lily

00:21:43.700 --> 00:21:48.070
and Lincoln along with us
on this leg of the tour.

00:21:48.070 --> 00:21:49.950
But just having a bus
like that and not having

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:53.849
to sleep in a van or out of a
crappy hotel or something was,

00:21:53.849 --> 00:21:54.930
we're spoiled.

00:21:54.930 --> 00:21:58.183
And I don't know if this is
necessarily the case with,

00:22:01.420 --> 00:22:03.450
like a band that would be going around.

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:04.900
I guess they do stop somewhere to shower

00:22:04.900 --> 00:22:06.917
but we do .

00:22:08.734 --> 00:22:10.760
You got that corn dog crust coming up.

00:22:10.760 --> 00:22:15.500
We do also, so basically,
as soon as the show's over,

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:17.524
we have our meet and greet,
we usually don't get out

00:22:17.524 --> 00:22:20.780
of the town and start, the
bus doesn't start rolling

00:22:20.780 --> 00:22:24.120
until after midnight, maybe 12:30.

00:22:24.120 --> 00:22:28.750
And then we roll into the next town,

00:22:28.750 --> 00:22:32.140
most of us go to sleep immediately
or within an hour or so

00:22:32.140 --> 00:22:34.590
and then you don't set
an alarm or anything,

00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:35.620
you just wake up.

00:22:35.620 --> 00:22:36.917
Well you said we roll into town

00:22:36.917 --> 00:22:38.330
and most of us go to sleep.

00:22:38.330 --> 00:22:40.600
No, once the bus starts
moving, we go to sleep

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:43.050
and then it's driving to the place.

00:22:43.050 --> 00:22:45.574
Oh roll out of town.
Roll out of town, yes.

00:22:45.574 --> 00:22:47.560
Like an hour after we leave,

00:22:47.560 --> 00:22:50.333
everybody's pretty much
asleep and so then,

00:22:51.380 --> 00:22:54.670
you don't set an alarm,
you just wake up at some,

00:22:54.670 --> 00:22:58.650
you go to bed that late, you
going to bed at one, 1:30.

00:22:58.650 --> 00:23:02.390
Most people are gonna wake up at nine, 10.

00:23:02.390 --> 00:23:04.210
I'm gonna wake up at 10 or 11.

00:23:04.210 --> 00:23:07.290
And then--
And realize the bus

00:23:07.290 --> 00:23:10.050
is no longer moving and that
everyone has vacated the bus

00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:11.190
except for me and my children.

00:23:11.190 --> 00:23:13.210
Usually the Neals are
the last off the bus.

00:23:13.210 --> 00:23:17.250
I was the last off the bus
one time but most of the time,

00:23:17.250 --> 00:23:19.630
you guys, I get up and
I see all your shoes,

00:23:19.630 --> 00:23:22.860
you're still there, I'm
like oh they're still there.

00:23:22.860 --> 00:23:25.232
But then there's Gary, our
tour manager has created

00:23:25.232 --> 00:23:29.620
basically a map or a very
simple set of directions

00:23:29.620 --> 00:23:31.950
for this is how you get to the hotel.

00:23:31.950 --> 00:23:34.410
Here's your hotel key, you do have a hotel

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:37.780
'cause you're gonna go
there and basically shower

00:23:37.780 --> 00:23:41.070
and we would just
basically shower and then--

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:42.841
We'd have a couple of
hours to do something.

00:23:42.841 --> 00:23:47.670
Go out and eat lunch and
then maybe walk around

00:23:47.670 --> 00:23:51.260
or if there are scooters,
we would scoot around town--

00:23:51.260 --> 00:23:52.120
Oh yeah.
For a couple of hours

00:23:52.120 --> 00:23:54.290
before we have to get back for
sound check and sign posters

00:23:54.290 --> 00:23:55.270
for the meet and greet and all that.

00:23:55.270 --> 00:23:57.760
I'll tell ya, Denver's
a good place to scooter.

00:23:57.760 --> 00:23:59.427
Very good.
They got this river there

00:23:59.427 --> 00:24:02.450
that's got rapids all
in it and then there's,

00:24:02.450 --> 00:24:05.940
it's fancy, all the paths
they got around this river.

00:24:05.940 --> 00:24:09.810
Yeah that was, I'd say
Denver was the scooter,

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:11.720
scooting highlight.
Yeah.

00:24:11.720 --> 00:24:13.990
Scooting capital of the
world, you might say.

00:24:13.990 --> 00:24:16.774
Scooter low-light was probably--

00:24:16.774 --> 00:24:19.848
Omaha.
Omaha I would say.

00:24:19.848 --> 00:24:22.872
More for Britton than for us because,

00:24:22.872 --> 00:24:24.850
man, he took a tumble.

00:24:24.850 --> 00:24:26.570
Well and I saw the whole
thing in slow motion.

00:24:26.570 --> 00:24:30.270
Now let me just say that
right before Britton

00:24:30.270 --> 00:24:31.407
had his incident on the scooter--

00:24:31.407 --> 00:24:32.523
He hit a hole.

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:35.770
I was thinking about, we were in Omaha

00:24:35.770 --> 00:24:39.440
and there's also a giant river,
is it the Missouri River,

00:24:39.440 --> 00:24:40.500
is that what that was?
Yeah.

00:24:40.500 --> 00:24:43.761
Coming through Omaha and
we're going all around

00:24:43.761 --> 00:24:45.920
and scooting up and down these paths

00:24:45.920 --> 00:24:47.740
and there's this little park next

00:24:47.740 --> 00:24:51.030
to the Conagra campus
there and we're going

00:24:51.030 --> 00:24:54.390
around this pond and I was
thinking after we'd been out

00:24:54.390 --> 00:24:56.497
for an hour and a half, I was like,

00:24:56.497 --> 00:24:59.240
I'm kinda surprised
that no one has fallen.

00:24:59.240 --> 00:25:01.920
And I actually in my mind
was like, kinda surprised

00:25:01.920 --> 00:25:03.700
that one of the kids, and
when I say one of the kids,

00:25:03.700 --> 00:25:06.840
I kinda throw Britton,
Britton's more a kid than--

00:25:06.840 --> 00:25:09.140
He's 19 but--
We could be his dad.

00:25:09.140 --> 00:25:12.246
Right, he's only five
years older than Lincoln.

00:25:12.246 --> 00:25:15.444
And I was like I'm surprised
that one of them hasn't fallen

00:25:15.444 --> 00:25:20.247
on these scooters yet and
literally three minutes later--

00:25:20.247 --> 00:25:21.830
You voodooed him.

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:22.663
I didn't say anything.

00:25:22.663 --> 00:25:23.970
Did you stab a doll?

00:25:23.970 --> 00:25:27.340
I'm going over this bridge in this park,

00:25:27.340 --> 00:25:30.390
this sort of arched
bridge and we get to the,

00:25:30.390 --> 00:25:33.114
and Britton's in front of me
and it was just this change

00:25:33.114 --> 00:25:35.610
in the surface, this little bump.

00:25:35.610 --> 00:25:36.627
Sometimes all it takes is a little bump.

00:25:36.627 --> 00:25:38.720
It was a hole 'cause I hit it too.

00:25:38.720 --> 00:25:40.840
Yeah there's a drop off.

00:25:40.840 --> 00:25:42.850
It was a drop off and then
a bump on the other side

00:25:42.850 --> 00:25:45.720
'cause it was like this
depressed area of bricks.

00:25:45.720 --> 00:25:49.830
And he hit it and I saw
him begin to lose control

00:25:49.830 --> 00:25:51.527
and then he over corrected
and then he came back

00:25:51.527 --> 00:25:54.550
and then he over corrected
again and then he flew off

00:25:54.550 --> 00:25:56.380
of of the scooter at full speed

00:25:56.380 --> 00:26:00.840
and did a pretty athletic
move to fall down and roll

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:03.990
into a back flip and
then land in the grass.

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:07.970
But I could see on his face
immediately that he was shaken.

00:26:07.970 --> 00:26:10.880
All I could see as I was
coming over the bridge horizon

00:26:10.880 --> 00:26:13.260
were his feet in the air.

00:26:13.260 --> 00:26:14.830
Oh so you saw that.
Yeah.

00:26:14.830 --> 00:26:16.870
And then he was rollin'.

00:26:16.870 --> 00:26:19.420
Tore up his elbow and one of his hands.

00:26:19.420 --> 00:26:22.300
It's like hold on man,
those are your money makers.

00:26:22.300 --> 00:26:24.128
You're a guitarist.
Right and he had a show

00:26:24.128 --> 00:26:27.958
and we had a show, well he
had a show the next night.

00:26:27.958 --> 00:26:30.540
Right because we didn't
have a show that night.

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:33.645
Now thankfully Jenna who
is here was also there

00:26:33.645 --> 00:26:36.470
at the time because Link
and I just proceeded

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:38.048
to laugh and make fun of Britton.

00:26:39.310 --> 00:26:41.263
As he just sat there and shook.

00:26:42.999 --> 00:26:44.550
And--

00:26:44.550 --> 00:26:46.520
We knew he wasn't dead.
He wasn't really hurt.

00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:47.980
He wasn't actively bleeding.

00:26:47.980 --> 00:26:49.140
There wasn't anything broken.

00:26:49.140 --> 00:26:50.330
Well he was actively bleeding.

00:26:50.330 --> 00:26:51.590
He was actively bleeding.

00:26:51.590 --> 00:26:54.000
In fact he got blood all over his pants.

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:56.950
He was shaken, he got a little pale.

00:26:56.950 --> 00:26:58.930
He was a little bit in shock but we knew

00:26:58.930 --> 00:27:02.320
that he wasn't permanently
injured so we laughed.

00:27:02.320 --> 00:27:04.790
And that's what guys do, man.

00:27:04.790 --> 00:27:09.090
And then Jenna came in
and was thinking straight

00:27:09.090 --> 00:27:10.710
and strategically.

00:27:10.710 --> 00:27:13.410
What was that magic fluid
you started pouring on his

00:27:15.570 --> 00:27:19.037
arm, like contact solution or something.

00:27:20.857 --> 00:27:23.860
It was just water.
Oh it was just water.

00:27:23.860 --> 00:27:25.430
Now she sacrificed--
Glasses cleaner.

00:27:25.430 --> 00:27:28.190
Her glasses cloth.

00:27:28.190 --> 00:27:30.730
Oh it was cloth, I thought the,

00:27:30.730 --> 00:27:32.710
when you said I'm gonna
use my glasses cleaner,

00:27:32.710 --> 00:27:36.130
I thought all of that
liquid up until this moment,

00:27:36.130 --> 00:27:39.060
I thought she was pouring
glasses cleaner all over his arm.

00:27:39.060 --> 00:27:40.730
I was like hmm that's like MacGyver.

00:27:40.730 --> 00:27:41.870
But that's also poison.

00:27:41.870 --> 00:27:43.993
I thought it was alcohol.

00:27:45.350 --> 00:27:47.940
No I think that like--
Which is why I was just

00:27:47.940 --> 00:27:49.570
sitting on the side laughing.

00:27:49.570 --> 00:27:52.130
'Cause who am I, who knows
what I would have poured

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:53.410
in his wound.
Right.

00:27:53.410 --> 00:27:55.570
Hey let me pee on it, man,
I think when you get stung

00:27:55.570 --> 00:27:57.070
by some asphalt, you gotta pee on it.

00:27:57.070 --> 00:27:59.588
Right yeah, like a jellyfish.

00:27:59.588 --> 00:28:00.730
But--
I probably could have

00:28:00.730 --> 00:28:01.940
convinced him, that would have been funny.

00:28:01.940 --> 00:28:05.410
Jenna took care of him
and we did all scoot back

00:28:05.410 --> 00:28:07.050
to a place where we could
drop the scooters off

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:11.260
'cause we were outside of
the scooter offloading area

00:28:11.260 --> 00:28:13.700
that we couldn't, the
app would not accept us

00:28:13.700 --> 00:28:15.490
to just stop where we were.

00:28:15.490 --> 00:28:16.570
But he ended up being okay.

00:28:16.570 --> 00:28:18.620
He looked kinda cool
playing the next night

00:28:18.620 --> 00:28:21.470
with a large bandage around his elbow.

00:28:21.470 --> 00:28:23.360
He got some sympathy points or whatever.

00:28:23.360 --> 00:28:25.013
But anyway in terms of the schedule,

00:28:25.013 --> 00:28:27.260
yeah we typically do
something for a couple hours.

00:28:27.260 --> 00:28:30.690
Usually no one would get hurt and then,

00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:34.070
we'd show up at the venue,
we'd do a soundcheck,

00:28:34.070 --> 00:28:36.414
we'd sign posters for the meet and greet

00:28:36.414 --> 00:28:38.800
and then we'd just sit backstage.

00:28:38.800 --> 00:28:42.220
We'd eat our food and so it ended up being

00:28:42.220 --> 00:28:45.100
like three hours of just
kinda sitting around backstage

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:47.463
and then doing the show and
the whole thing starts over.

00:28:48.830 --> 00:28:50.710
But you would really
get into the rhythm of,

00:28:50.710 --> 00:28:53.355
I can't wait to get back to the bus.

00:28:53.355 --> 00:28:56.100
I know that was Lily's favorite part.

00:28:56.100 --> 00:28:58.070
There's something about
climbing on this bus

00:28:58.070 --> 00:29:01.180
and at some points the
bunks were three high

00:29:01.180 --> 00:29:03.440
and at some points they were two high.

00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:06.110
When they're three high you
cannot sit up in your bunk.

00:29:06.110 --> 00:29:09.650
You just gotta roll in and
roll out like a log roll.

00:29:09.650 --> 00:29:12.360
But we got the two, they gave me and you--

00:29:12.360 --> 00:29:13.893
I still could--
The two high.

00:29:13.893 --> 00:29:16.543
Yeah and some of the
crew but the youngsters

00:29:16.543 --> 00:29:18.113
got the three high.

00:29:21.220 --> 00:29:24.380
There is something like cozy,
it's like a cozy hideout

00:29:24.380 --> 00:29:25.213
and you lay down in the thing

00:29:25.213 --> 00:29:29.120
and then you pull your curtain
closed and you Velcro it

00:29:29.120 --> 00:29:31.720
and then you can take your pants off.

00:29:31.720 --> 00:29:32.553
Hmm.

00:29:32.553 --> 00:29:34.730
Take whatever you got
on off if you want to.

00:29:34.730 --> 00:29:35.590
I wasn't naked.

00:29:35.590 --> 00:29:37.380
I had the really comfortable pants though

00:29:37.380 --> 00:29:40.800
that I left on 'cause
it's almost like a blanket

00:29:40.800 --> 00:29:41.690
in and of itself.

00:29:41.690 --> 00:29:43.110
There was a little light in there,

00:29:43.110 --> 00:29:45.660
then you can set up your
earphones and you can listen

00:29:45.660 --> 00:29:50.190
to some, a playlist to help you sleep.

00:29:50.190 --> 00:29:52.280
Or you can just go to sleep to the murmur

00:29:52.280 --> 00:29:54.680
and the rustle of the bus.

00:29:54.680 --> 00:29:59.120
And that was a lot of fun
but I started playing,

00:29:59.120 --> 00:30:01.293
well every night I would go to sleep with,

00:30:02.650 --> 00:30:07.370
I downloaded this playlist
on Spotify called Deep Sleep.

00:30:07.370 --> 00:30:09.170
And I would listen to that thing.

00:30:09.170 --> 00:30:12.685
And it was like eight or nine hours

00:30:12.685 --> 00:30:17.685
of just ambience, sleep-inducing--

00:30:21.602 --> 00:30:22.697
And then some pianos over top of that.

00:30:22.697 --> 00:30:25.938
And what headphones are
you employing for this?

00:30:25.938 --> 00:30:28.960
My Bose noise-canceling in ear headphones,

00:30:28.960 --> 00:30:29.793
not a sponsor.

00:30:29.793 --> 00:30:31.140
Right 'cause you can't use the AirPods

00:30:31.140 --> 00:30:32.360
'cause they would run out eventually.

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:34.230
I actually did use AirPods sometime,

00:30:34.230 --> 00:30:35.700
and they would just fall out of my ears

00:30:35.700 --> 00:30:36.800
and then I'd wake up in
the middle of the night

00:30:36.800 --> 00:30:39.213
searching for an AirPod
because I'd be too anxious

00:30:39.213 --> 00:30:40.780
that I'd lost it forever.

00:30:40.780 --> 00:30:44.910
So does this preclude you
from sleeping on your side?

00:30:44.910 --> 00:30:47.210
I would roll all over and
sometimes the whole cord

00:30:47.210 --> 00:30:48.377
would just tangle around
my waist like a belt.

00:30:48.377 --> 00:30:50.810
But what about ear pain when you,

00:30:50.810 --> 00:30:53.140
I'm an exclusive side sleeper.

00:30:53.140 --> 00:30:55.860
I'm a back sleeper but I
did sleep on my side some

00:30:55.860 --> 00:30:58.764
and there is inner ear pain
where you're smooshing it in.

00:30:58.764 --> 00:31:00.950
That's why I can't listen
to things in the ears.

00:31:00.950 --> 00:31:02.800
That's not the worst
thing about it though.

00:31:02.800 --> 00:31:05.040
But I will tell you what is.

00:31:05.040 --> 00:31:08.410
And that's when you're listening
to an eight hour playlist

00:31:08.410 --> 00:31:12.450
of just instrumental
ambient music which is great

00:31:12.450 --> 00:31:14.930
to send you into a deep stupor.

00:31:14.930 --> 00:31:17.330
But you only do this while traveling.

00:31:17.330 --> 00:31:19.630
You don't do this at home.
I do not.

00:31:19.630 --> 00:31:22.913
And I'm laying there sleeping
and then all of a sudden,

00:31:24.450 --> 00:31:27.513
a woman says to me,

00:31:29.007 --> 00:31:30.137
"Don't leave me,"

00:31:31.300 --> 00:31:34.993
and I woke up terrified.

00:31:36.110 --> 00:31:39.195
And I look around, there's no woman.

00:31:39.195 --> 00:31:40.028
Right.

00:31:41.285 --> 00:31:45.200
There's nobody talking on
this deep sleep playlist.

00:31:45.200 --> 00:31:48.000
Much less singing, this is instrumental.

00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:53.000
And I'm like, I dreamt that
a woman said don't leave me.

00:31:53.360 --> 00:31:54.440
I went back to sleep.

00:31:54.440 --> 00:31:55.370
Was it Christy's voice?

00:31:55.370 --> 00:31:59.260
But I was kinda, it was
more dramatic than that.

00:31:59.260 --> 00:32:01.810
It was a dramatic Christy--
It was a dramatic,

00:32:02.860 --> 00:32:05.783
I don't know, it was a lot of need in it.

00:32:08.060 --> 00:32:10.030
It was very disconcerting

00:32:10.030 --> 00:32:12.010
and I don't remember dreaming anything

00:32:12.010 --> 00:32:14.350
but I was able to go back to sleep

00:32:14.350 --> 00:32:17.340
and then I went deep again, nothing.

00:32:17.340 --> 00:32:18.803
Wake up next day fine.

00:32:19.810 --> 00:32:22.030
Fast forward to the next
night, same routine,

00:32:22.030 --> 00:32:25.100
I go to sleep, I've been
asleep for a few hours.

00:32:25.100 --> 00:32:28.683
And then all of a sudden, the woman.

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:31.233
Don't leave me.

00:32:32.330 --> 00:32:35.615
She returned and this time
I woke up and I'm like,

00:32:35.615 --> 00:32:39.040
okay, I know, is there a ghost in there?

00:32:39.040 --> 00:32:41.470
I don't know what's going on and I'm like

00:32:41.470 --> 00:32:43.440
it's the frickin' playlist.

00:32:43.440 --> 00:32:45.630
In the middle of a nine hour playlist

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:49.080
that's just instrumental
there is one sound snippet

00:32:49.930 --> 00:32:53.777
in the middle of a song
where a woman says,

00:32:53.777 --> 00:32:57.650
"Don't leave me," and that is terrifying.

00:32:57.650 --> 00:32:59.060
It's a--

00:32:59.060 --> 00:33:02.857
Spotify, you gotta remove
that from the playlist,

00:33:02.857 --> 00:33:04.630
and a matter of fact, I found it,

00:33:04.630 --> 00:33:06.910
because the third time I was like,

00:33:06.910 --> 00:33:09.360
you know when this happens,
I'm gonna take a screenshot

00:33:09.360 --> 00:33:13.000
and I'm gonna remember so I
can remember where this is.

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:14.130
It's just an Easter egg, man.

00:33:14.130 --> 00:33:16.210
It's an Easter egg that
people who commit fully

00:33:16.210 --> 00:33:17.710
to the playlist experience.

00:33:17.710 --> 00:33:20.250
Allana Johnson, Breathing Star.

00:33:20.250 --> 00:33:21.448
Yeah it's Allana.

00:33:24.036 --> 00:33:26.119
Don't leave me.

00:33:27.840 --> 00:33:29.970
I like to feel needed and wanted.

00:33:29.970 --> 00:33:31.370
Not when you're in a deep sleep, man,

00:33:31.370 --> 00:33:34.240
it's like what, I'm not leaving you, girl.

00:33:34.240 --> 00:33:35.633
I'm laying here asleep!

00:33:38.320 --> 00:33:39.663
You freak me out, man.

00:33:40.760 --> 00:33:43.910
So then I told the people
at the show that night

00:33:43.910 --> 00:33:45.790
and then there was a good
runner about don't leave me.

00:33:45.790 --> 00:33:47.377
Yeah it really worked, it did.

00:33:47.377 --> 00:33:49.980
That's the type of stuff
you live for, you know,

00:33:49.980 --> 00:33:51.443
getting scared, the crap scared out of me.

00:33:51.443 --> 00:33:53.810
What I was thinking was
gonna happen is the music

00:33:53.810 --> 00:33:56.127
was gonna drop out completely
and some woman was gonna say,

00:33:56.127 --> 00:33:58.670
"Don't leave me."

00:33:58.670 --> 00:34:00.028
And then it was gonna come back in

00:34:00.028 --> 00:34:03.100
'cause that would wake me up for sure.

00:34:03.100 --> 00:34:05.530
So that was a low-light
but I would say a highlight

00:34:05.530 --> 00:34:08.970
of the tour besides again like I said,

00:34:08.970 --> 00:34:12.710
we're so spoiled by, it was
just a great audience reception

00:34:12.710 --> 00:34:14.900
and it was great to connect
with so many Mythical Beasts

00:34:14.900 --> 00:34:19.900
and to get even more of an
idea of what makes them tick

00:34:20.150 --> 00:34:22.743
and what makes them laugh, you know.

00:34:25.010 --> 00:34:28.615
It turned out to be very
special for me because

00:34:28.615 --> 00:34:31.030
Lily and Lincoln were there.

00:34:31.030 --> 00:34:32.680
I didn't think Lily was
gonna be able to come

00:34:32.680 --> 00:34:34.480
but then the fact that they
both were able to come,

00:34:34.480 --> 00:34:38.683
we should say that Locke was gonna come

00:34:38.683 --> 00:34:40.866
but then because of--

00:34:40.866 --> 00:34:44.980
He had like five basketball
games in that 11 week period

00:34:44.980 --> 00:34:48.630
so he would have left his boys hanging.

00:34:48.630 --> 00:34:50.023
Yeah he couldn't do it.

00:34:50.988 --> 00:34:54.550
But he was obviously invited
and there was a chance

00:34:54.550 --> 00:34:57.210
he may come and then
hopefully in the future

00:34:57.210 --> 00:34:58.560
he'll be able to do something like that

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:01.320
because it was so special
for me and my kids,

00:35:01.320 --> 00:35:02.440
and of course Lando's too young,

00:35:02.440 --> 00:35:03.710
Shepherd's probably too young

00:35:03.710 --> 00:35:06.512
because they need to be supervised more.

00:35:06.512 --> 00:35:07.710
They have to fend for themselves.

00:35:07.710 --> 00:35:10.059
The kids have to fend for themselves.

00:35:10.059 --> 00:35:15.059
But you know, I realized
that it was such a sweet

00:35:17.450 --> 00:35:20.330
and special time for me and
Lily and Lincoln afterward

00:35:20.330 --> 00:35:22.120
when I started to reflect
on the fact that really

00:35:22.120 --> 00:35:24.650
the only time that we spend
that much time together,

00:35:24.650 --> 00:35:29.650
like every waking hour
for 10 days straight,

00:35:30.640 --> 00:35:33.780
the only time we do that is
if we go on a family vacation.

00:35:33.780 --> 00:35:34.613
Right.
You know,

00:35:34.613 --> 00:35:36.940
and a family vacation is
a totally different vibe

00:35:36.940 --> 00:35:41.940
than being on tour because
everybody's in a different mode

00:35:42.480 --> 00:35:45.861
when vacation is happening
but when we were on tour,

00:35:45.861 --> 00:35:48.840
we're in tour mode so we're
working and we're doing things

00:35:48.840 --> 00:35:53.170
and I think they kinda
rose to the challenge,

00:35:53.170 --> 00:35:55.010
I wouldn't call it a
challenge but they kinda rose

00:35:55.010 --> 00:35:59.625
to the occasion of hey,
we're part of the crew.

00:35:59.625 --> 00:36:02.260
They rolled some posters
and they would hand 'em out

00:36:02.260 --> 00:36:03.093
at the meet and greets.

00:36:03.093 --> 00:36:06.050
They didn't roll posters
every night, sorry Jenna.

00:36:06.050 --> 00:36:09.910
But they felt, they were treated as equals

00:36:09.910 --> 00:36:13.060
and I could definitely
see that in their takeaway

00:36:13.060 --> 00:36:15.080
as they reported back to Christy and Lando

00:36:15.080 --> 00:36:17.090
when we got back home
how much they enjoyed it

00:36:17.090 --> 00:36:19.143
and they talked about tour life.

00:36:20.120 --> 00:36:24.890
I was hopeful but I didn't
wanna be too hopeful,

00:36:24.890 --> 00:36:27.976
you know me, I think,
oh when they get back,

00:36:27.976 --> 00:36:30.367
they're gonna have this
perfect idea of what tour was

00:36:30.367 --> 00:36:32.450
and it's gonna be the
most influential thing

00:36:32.450 --> 00:36:34.920
in their entire lives so
I didn't wanna go into

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:37.330
full dad Link mode with my expectations

00:36:37.330 --> 00:36:42.330
so I was pleasantly surprised
that it meant so much

00:36:42.710 --> 00:36:46.700
to them and they said
they had a great time

00:36:46.700 --> 00:36:51.470
and I think just spending
that amount of time together,

00:36:51.470 --> 00:36:55.210
you really get to, again, when
you're not in vacation mode,

00:36:55.210 --> 00:36:59.390
it was a great opportunity for
us to get to know each other,

00:36:59.390 --> 00:37:02.410
'cause I was able to
observe them interacting

00:37:02.410 --> 00:37:04.900
with the other members
of the crew, and, okay,

00:37:04.900 --> 00:37:07.210
when do they choose to pipe
up and what do they choose

00:37:07.210 --> 00:37:10.370
to say and what are their
perspectives on things.

00:37:10.370 --> 00:37:11.960
It's something that I
don't have the benefit of

00:37:11.960 --> 00:37:13.860
that often and actually thought about,

00:37:15.400 --> 00:37:17.590
I got a little jealous
of Christy over the years

00:37:17.590 --> 00:37:22.590
who as difficult and
demanding as it was for her

00:37:22.890 --> 00:37:25.470
to homeschool the kids
for all those years,

00:37:25.470 --> 00:37:26.760
they've been at public school for,

00:37:26.760 --> 00:37:30.260
this is their third year now
but and the same with Jessie,

00:37:30.260 --> 00:37:34.340
it's like, I got to see
a little bit of a side

00:37:34.340 --> 00:37:38.320
of them that I think Christy
and I'm guessing Jessie

00:37:38.320 --> 00:37:40.270
got to see of our kids
that we don't get to see

00:37:40.270 --> 00:37:43.727
as much when it's not like
you're constantly with them.

00:37:43.727 --> 00:37:44.870
You know?

00:37:44.870 --> 00:37:47.167
And then at the end of 11
days, then you're good.

00:37:49.424 --> 00:37:50.257
You don't have to do it anymore.

00:37:50.257 --> 00:37:51.090
Yeah right.

00:37:52.360 --> 00:37:54.610
But it just turned out to
be a very special time.

00:37:54.610 --> 00:37:56.510
And you know what, they said
that they enjoyed getting

00:37:56.510 --> 00:37:58.930
to know you more in kind of a work mode.

00:37:58.930 --> 00:38:01.010
Unprompted, they were like, you know what,

00:38:01.010 --> 00:38:02.830
I actually have a different view of Rhett,

00:38:02.830 --> 00:38:05.153
and I'm like, and I told
them about my vacation theory

00:38:05.153 --> 00:38:07.730
and I extended, I was
like, yeah a lot of times

00:38:07.730 --> 00:38:10.630
when you see Rhett it's in family

00:38:10.630 --> 00:38:12.230
or if we do vacations together,

00:38:12.230 --> 00:38:14.620
it's in that type of mode
and it was just like,

00:38:14.620 --> 00:38:17.050
the thing that, I think
it was Lincoln who said,

00:38:17.050 --> 00:38:20.190
he was like, maybe it was Lily.

00:38:20.190 --> 00:38:22.730
She was like, you know what, you guys,

00:38:22.730 --> 00:38:24.380
I thought it was cool
to get to see you guys

00:38:24.380 --> 00:38:26.470
actually enjoy hanging
out with each other.

00:38:26.470 --> 00:38:28.280
I was like well have you
ever watched our show?

00:38:28.280 --> 00:38:30.457
But I think she knows
that that's the show.

00:38:30.457 --> 00:38:32.970
But it's not a show, I
think is what she said.

00:38:32.970 --> 00:38:34.890
I enjoyed you guys enjoying each other.

00:38:34.890 --> 00:38:37.316
Well I think--

00:38:37.316 --> 00:38:40.551
I didn't know what I thought
about, my first thought

00:38:40.551 --> 00:38:42.710
when they were gonna
go, I was like, oh no,

00:38:42.710 --> 00:38:46.124
we're gonna have to worry about them

00:38:46.124 --> 00:38:50.270
and not be able to get into
places because they're too young

00:38:50.270 --> 00:38:51.750
and et cetera, et cetera,

00:38:51.750 --> 00:38:54.552
but my thought was--

00:38:54.552 --> 00:38:55.385
You talkin' about strip clubs again?

00:38:55.385 --> 00:38:57.350
Yeah yeah yeah.

00:38:57.350 --> 00:38:58.183
My thought--

00:38:58.183 --> 00:38:59.730
I've never been in a strip
club just for the record.

00:38:59.730 --> 00:39:00.563
That was a joke.

00:39:00.563 --> 00:39:01.847
My thought was--

00:39:03.090 --> 00:39:05.000
No judgment if you have, hey listen,

00:39:05.000 --> 00:39:06.770
let's change the subject.
I'm just--

00:39:06.770 --> 00:39:07.820
Why you talkin' about strip clubs?

00:39:07.820 --> 00:39:10.530
I didn't say it, didn't
say anything about that.

00:39:10.530 --> 00:39:12.363
I was talking about bars.

00:39:13.212 --> 00:39:15.790
18 and up or 21 and up, you know.

00:39:15.790 --> 00:39:19.060
There's some establishments
where children are not allowed.

00:39:19.060 --> 00:39:19.960
Right.
Right.

00:39:21.305 --> 00:39:25.490
So but I actually think that having,

00:39:25.490 --> 00:39:28.520
and I think that Britton also
contributed to this as well.

00:39:28.520 --> 00:39:29.720
Yeah.

00:39:29.720 --> 00:39:33.030
Having younger people there

00:39:33.030 --> 00:39:36.410
definitely significantly
changes the dynamic

00:39:36.410 --> 00:39:39.635
'cause we have a tendency to just be

00:39:39.635 --> 00:39:44.100
in work mode like all the time.

00:39:44.100 --> 00:39:45.840
Now sure, oh let's go
get on these scooters

00:39:45.840 --> 00:39:49.740
and run around, if it was just me and you

00:39:49.740 --> 00:39:51.707
and it has been just
me and you and gone out

00:39:51.707 --> 00:39:54.081
and just scooted around or whatever,

00:39:54.081 --> 00:39:56.880
but I think that something
about having kids there,

00:39:56.880 --> 00:39:59.610
it lightens the atmosphere a little bit.

00:39:59.610 --> 00:40:01.460
It's not that we don't know how
to have fun with each other,

00:40:01.460 --> 00:40:05.730
it's just that we have
so many things going on

00:40:05.730 --> 00:40:07.930
that there's at any moment we could stop

00:40:07.930 --> 00:40:12.820
and start talking about
some work-related thing

00:40:12.820 --> 00:40:15.920
or some project and even
if it's a creative thing,

00:40:15.920 --> 00:40:18.600
we'll do it in a very serious manner,

00:40:18.600 --> 00:40:20.343
and so I think that they
actually kinda change

00:40:20.343 --> 00:40:24.370
the dynamic, the group
dynamic, just being kids,

00:40:24.370 --> 00:40:26.390
just being silly.

00:40:26.390 --> 00:40:28.930
Yeah and kinda having
this vibe of wanting them

00:40:28.930 --> 00:40:32.050
to have a good time so
electing to do more things

00:40:32.050 --> 00:40:35.330
to have a good time versus
just maybe I'll just sit

00:40:35.330 --> 00:40:37.920
in the hotel or let's talk about work.

00:40:37.920 --> 00:40:40.480
And I do wanna say I really
do appreciate the way

00:40:40.480 --> 00:40:45.360
that you included them
and it was unfortunate

00:40:45.360 --> 00:40:48.050
but totally understandable
that Locke wasn't able

00:40:48.050 --> 00:40:51.620
to come but the fact that you
were able to extend yourself

00:40:51.620 --> 00:40:52.990
to include them as much as you did,

00:40:52.990 --> 00:40:55.760
they without me asking, they noticed

00:40:55.760 --> 00:40:57.460
and I really appreciated that too.

00:40:57.460 --> 00:41:01.280
I thought that was very gracious and yeah,

00:41:01.280 --> 00:41:04.400
I think fun was had by everyone

00:41:04.400 --> 00:41:07.010
except for maybe Britton's elbow.

00:41:07.010 --> 00:41:10.240
And I think it's cool just in conclusion

00:41:10.240 --> 00:41:14.410
that a lot of people ask us,

00:41:14.410 --> 00:41:16.210
what do your kids think about what you do?

00:41:16.210 --> 00:41:18.920
And it was just, it meant a lot to me

00:41:18.920 --> 00:41:23.500
that they thought it was
fun and cool to be a part

00:41:23.500 --> 00:41:26.265
of something their dad
was doing, you know?

00:41:26.265 --> 00:41:29.400
There weren't any eye rolls.

00:41:29.400 --> 00:41:31.110
It was like hey, this is--

00:41:31.110 --> 00:41:33.602
And that--
This is cool.

00:41:33.602 --> 00:41:37.310
I do want, and if Locke can't
go in the next couple years

00:41:37.310 --> 00:41:40.163
or whatever then hopefully
Shepherd will be able to.

00:41:41.190 --> 00:41:45.130
I do want them to be able
to experience it because...

00:41:47.634 --> 00:41:50.410
Yeah, it's like, there's a different feel

00:41:50.410 --> 00:41:52.080
than just saying oh I'm gonna come on set

00:41:52.080 --> 00:41:54.030
when you have a guest
that I'm interested in

00:41:54.030 --> 00:41:57.533
or something like that,
it's a different thing.

00:41:58.438 --> 00:41:59.800
I'm glad that--
We'll see.

00:41:59.800 --> 00:42:02.200
We had the opportunity
to give 'em that gift

00:42:02.200 --> 00:42:05.080
and I believe that they'll
remember that trip forever

00:42:05.080 --> 00:42:06.580
and that's what I hope for so.

00:42:07.570 --> 00:42:11.140
That was my highlight but I
wanna hear about your vacation

00:42:11.140 --> 00:42:13.823
too because we haven't
had a chance to talk.

00:42:14.725 --> 00:42:17.630
Yeah so we got through with the tour.

00:42:17.630 --> 00:42:21.832
I flew directly from
Minneapolis to the beach

00:42:21.832 --> 00:42:25.923
in North Carolina to spend
time with my wife's family.

00:42:27.050 --> 00:42:31.363
Her parents and her sister
and husband and kids.

00:42:33.090 --> 00:42:38.090
And we were going to spend a
couple days in one location

00:42:38.430 --> 00:42:40.803
and a few days in another location.

00:42:41.760 --> 00:42:45.400
So you may, if you
followed me on Instagram,

00:42:45.400 --> 00:42:46.843
shout-out to @rhettmc on Instagram,

00:42:46.843 --> 00:42:50.103
a very dynamic Instagram
account from time to time.

00:42:51.790 --> 00:42:56.790
You may have seen my,
what went down on the day

00:42:56.832 --> 00:42:58.470
of July 4th.

00:42:58.470 --> 00:43:00.152
I don't know if you saw any of this.

00:43:00.152 --> 00:43:01.569
The only thing I saw was
you looking for horses

00:43:01.569 --> 00:43:04.020
which you can leave for Instagram.

00:43:04.020 --> 00:43:05.960
After that I was like okay.

00:43:05.960 --> 00:43:08.400
Oh you missed, you missed,

00:43:08.400 --> 00:43:10.700
that was just the warmup story.

00:43:10.700 --> 00:43:12.310
You didn't see the fireworks show?

00:43:12.310 --> 00:43:13.550
Uh-uh.

00:43:13.550 --> 00:43:16.431
Oh brother.

00:43:16.431 --> 00:43:19.060
Okay so I've talked about this before.

00:43:19.060 --> 00:43:24.060
I've talked about how my
father-in-law used to get firework,

00:43:26.490 --> 00:43:28.310
together, he would produce the show,

00:43:28.310 --> 00:43:30.770
i.e. he would finance the show
and then I would go down to--

00:43:30.770 --> 00:43:32.370
He would executive produce.
I would go down

00:43:32.370 --> 00:43:36.820
to South Carolina and buy the fireworks

00:43:36.820 --> 00:43:39.430
and then spend all day
wiring them together

00:43:39.430 --> 00:43:42.062
with extra fuses and stuff,

00:43:42.062 --> 00:43:47.062
creating as crazy of an
amateur fireworks show

00:43:47.640 --> 00:43:49.240
as you can get away with, right?

00:43:50.645 --> 00:43:52.830
Illegally in North Carolina.

00:43:52.830 --> 00:43:55.730
Well, it's illegal in
the sense that going 62

00:43:55.730 --> 00:43:59.470
in a 55 is or 64 in a 55 is because--

00:43:59.470 --> 00:44:00.900
Okay.

00:44:00.900 --> 00:44:02.622
No.
Okay.

00:44:02.622 --> 00:44:04.570
On July 4th--
They turn a blind eye.

00:44:04.570 --> 00:44:07.410
They turn a blind eye, in
fact, I've had the cops

00:44:07.410 --> 00:44:10.690
and when I say cops in this
setting, I mean C-O-P-S,

00:44:10.690 --> 00:44:14.910
drive by while I was in
the process of lighting

00:44:14.910 --> 00:44:19.560
a crap ton of fireworks, lighting them.

00:44:19.560 --> 00:44:21.699
And they just kinda like nod at ya,

00:44:21.699 --> 00:44:22.960
it's like oh it's Fourth of July.

00:44:22.960 --> 00:44:23.793
So--

00:44:23.793 --> 00:44:25.350
You're American, I'm American.

00:44:25.350 --> 00:44:26.760
Aw man I'll have to show you.

00:44:26.760 --> 00:44:28.620
I've still got video of some of it

00:44:28.620 --> 00:44:33.620
because we were renting
a, we were staying at

00:44:34.133 --> 00:44:36.920
one of my father-in-law's
houses that he's got down there.

00:44:36.920 --> 00:44:39.860
They have a property company
that's got different houses

00:44:39.860 --> 00:44:42.710
that you can rent so we
kinda reserved one of them.

00:44:42.710 --> 00:44:44.260
And it was one that was on the sound

00:44:44.260 --> 00:44:46.790
that had a very long dock
that went out into the sound.

00:44:46.790 --> 00:44:48.150
I was like this is perfect,

00:44:48.150 --> 00:44:51.570
we're gonna take all the
fireworks out onto the dock

00:44:51.570 --> 00:44:52.440
and light them.

00:44:52.440 --> 00:44:53.520
Oh no.

00:44:53.520 --> 00:44:56.660
But I was like I'm not gonna take all day

00:44:56.660 --> 00:44:58.530
to get the extra fuse,

00:44:58.530 --> 00:45:00.210
I was like just get me a powerful lighter

00:45:00.210 --> 00:45:04.363
that is a wind-proof
like torch kinda lighter

00:45:04.363 --> 00:45:06.820
and then we'll just figure
it out in the moment.

00:45:06.820 --> 00:45:08.517
You know just take some
stuff out there and light it

00:45:08.517 --> 00:45:10.390
and then string some stuff together.

00:45:10.390 --> 00:45:12.700
You're not used to all that prep anymore.

00:45:12.700 --> 00:45:15.080
You don't work hard anymore.
I'm not gonna do the work.

00:45:15.080 --> 00:45:19.620
And also I had Locke and
then his two teenage cousins

00:45:19.620 --> 00:45:23.150
and then my brother-in-law
so we had a lot of help

00:45:23.150 --> 00:45:27.739
to get the things out on
the dock and first of all,

00:45:27.739 --> 00:45:31.166
I don't remember exactly
how much money we spent

00:45:31.166 --> 00:45:34.748
on fireworks 10 years ago which
is the last time I did this

00:45:34.748 --> 00:45:37.520
but my father-in-law
went a little bit nuts

00:45:37.520 --> 00:45:38.810
with the amount of stuff that he bought.

00:45:38.810 --> 00:45:41.000
He sent one of his employees
down to South Carolina.

00:45:41.000 --> 00:45:45.150
The guy came back and
he had like a twin cab

00:45:45.150 --> 00:45:47.520
or like quad cab truck or whatever

00:45:47.520 --> 00:45:52.370
and the whole backseat was
just stacked to the ceiling

00:45:52.370 --> 00:45:54.508
with fireworks, like this guy
had of been in an accident

00:45:54.508 --> 00:45:56.260
and there was a little spark--

00:45:56.260 --> 00:45:57.320
Oh my gosh.

00:45:57.320 --> 00:46:00.180
He would have created a crater.

00:46:00.180 --> 00:46:01.288
Is he a smoker?

00:46:01.288 --> 00:46:04.790
No not a smoker, he wasn't
a smoker on that day.

00:46:04.790 --> 00:46:07.890
But anyway we set these
things off and the cool thing

00:46:07.890 --> 00:46:11.870
about setting the
fireworks off in the sound

00:46:11.870 --> 00:46:13.970
is you've got all these people watching

00:46:13.970 --> 00:46:16.210
from, of course, we've got
my family that's up there

00:46:16.210 --> 00:46:19.110
on the deck watching from the house

00:46:19.110 --> 00:46:20.790
and then you've got everyone
else that just come out

00:46:20.790 --> 00:46:23.260
to the back of their
houses all on the sound,

00:46:23.260 --> 00:46:26.247
on both sides, to watch all
the fireworks happening.

00:46:26.247 --> 00:46:27.220
Oh.
So by about--

00:46:27.220 --> 00:46:28.060
Did you warn 'em?

00:46:28.060 --> 00:46:31.990
No no, by about the
second round of lighting

00:46:31.990 --> 00:46:34.938
a couple of these, they
basically come in these boxes

00:46:34.938 --> 00:46:38.290
of fireworks that have, it's
just a box with a fuse on it

00:46:38.290 --> 00:46:41.360
and it has got a bunch
of mortars built into it.

00:46:41.360 --> 00:46:44.680
Sometimes it's 10 shots,
sometimes it's 40 shots, right?

00:46:44.680 --> 00:46:47.974
Anywhere in between, it
basically is its own little show.

00:46:47.974 --> 00:46:50.510
We had like 50 of these things.

00:46:51.670 --> 00:46:54.177
So there were times where
we were lining up six

00:46:54.177 --> 00:46:57.000
and twisting two fuses together and three

00:46:57.000 --> 00:47:00.060
of us would light 'em at
the same time and then run.

00:47:00.060 --> 00:47:04.530
And by the time we did the
second round of these things,

00:47:04.530 --> 00:47:06.691
we had an audience.

00:47:06.691 --> 00:47:10.313
People can tell when you've gotten through

00:47:10.313 --> 00:47:12.450
with a little bit, one round, right.

00:47:12.450 --> 00:47:14.540
It's like last firework goes
off and then it gets quiet

00:47:14.540 --> 00:47:17.460
and then all of a sudden
all these people from wider

00:47:17.460 --> 00:47:21.000
and wider radius would go, woo hoo hoo!

00:47:22.365 --> 00:47:23.198
Woo!

00:47:24.670 --> 00:47:26.650
And then we had an audience.

00:47:26.650 --> 00:47:28.718
That sounds like a rebel yell.

00:47:28.718 --> 00:47:31.320
And then there's other
people doing fireworks

00:47:31.320 --> 00:47:32.860
at their houses.

00:47:32.860 --> 00:47:34.670
But then by about the
fourth, and first of all,

00:47:34.670 --> 00:47:37.890
there were like 15 rounds that we did

00:47:37.890 --> 00:47:39.593
but by the third or fourth round,

00:47:40.480 --> 00:47:44.307
one guy yells out, "You win!"

00:47:46.770 --> 00:47:47.603
It's funny.
And I was like

00:47:47.603 --> 00:47:48.780
that's all I needed to hear.

00:47:48.780 --> 00:47:49.913
It's funny, yeah.

00:47:51.068 --> 00:47:54.020
That's specifically what
you were after, right?

00:47:54.020 --> 00:47:55.610
You win.

00:47:55.610 --> 00:47:58.503
Not that's awesome, he said
exactly what you wanted to hear.

00:47:58.503 --> 00:48:01.893
Well no I told my
father-in-law, I was like,

00:48:02.943 --> 00:48:05.920
when the guy said all
the things, I was like,

00:48:05.920 --> 00:48:08.410
we are going to have
the best firework show.

00:48:08.410 --> 00:48:09.840
We're gonna win fireworks, boys.

00:48:09.840 --> 00:48:10.673
In this area.

00:48:11.820 --> 00:48:13.850
Non-professional, that is.

00:48:13.850 --> 00:48:17.940
It's funny you talk about
the woos because last year,

00:48:17.940 --> 00:48:21.880
we're watching fireworks from our house,

00:48:21.880 --> 00:48:24.930
from our bedroom deck, we just happened to

00:48:24.930 --> 00:48:27.660
through some trees see
the fireworks display

00:48:27.660 --> 00:48:29.830
of the local high school.

00:48:29.830 --> 00:48:34.010
And we realized and we ran upstairs.

00:48:34.010 --> 00:48:35.390
This year the kids watched 'em

00:48:35.390 --> 00:48:37.510
but we were hanging out downstairs,

00:48:37.510 --> 00:48:39.920
we were with some friends
and you seen one firework,

00:48:39.920 --> 00:48:40.770
you seen 'em all.

00:48:42.243 --> 00:48:46.150
I'm sorry that that's my
attitude but last year--

00:48:46.150 --> 00:48:47.650
Yeah you didn't see my--
We were so elated

00:48:47.650 --> 00:48:50.090
because fireworks started
going off and it was me,

00:48:50.090 --> 00:48:53.530
Christy and the kids up there
and all of a sudden behind me,

00:48:53.530 --> 00:48:55.103
I just hear woo!

00:48:57.350 --> 00:48:59.513
And I turn around and Christy's like what?

00:49:01.310 --> 00:49:03.434
I'm from Kinston, man.
Yeah.

00:49:03.434 --> 00:49:06.470
I'm like and that's almost down
to the beach where you were

00:49:06.470 --> 00:49:08.670
is where she grew up
and it's like that's a,

00:49:10.614 --> 00:49:13.180
that's just an instinctive southern thing

00:49:13.180 --> 00:49:15.900
when you see something on fire in the sky.

00:49:15.900 --> 00:49:17.330
Woo!

00:49:17.330 --> 00:49:19.390
Yeah well here's the thing,

00:49:19.390 --> 00:49:21.940
the reason it's, not only is it better

00:49:21.940 --> 00:49:24.000
than a professional show
because you're the one

00:49:24.000 --> 00:49:27.040
in control of it, but
you're so close to it.

00:49:27.040 --> 00:49:28.702
Let me--
So unsafe.

00:49:28.702 --> 00:49:29.535
I'm gonna show,

00:49:29.535 --> 00:49:32.003
this is Locke filming
us lighting one round.

00:49:34.925 --> 00:49:36.008
Whoa!

00:49:37.650 --> 00:49:38.993
This is all on Instagram.

00:49:41.344 --> 00:49:42.177
Good gracious.

00:49:43.199 --> 00:49:44.253
Oh my word.

00:49:45.810 --> 00:49:46.747
He's filming--

00:49:48.166 --> 00:49:49.410
The wrong part of it.

00:49:49.410 --> 00:49:51.360
He's filming the gazebo.

00:49:51.360 --> 00:49:52.860
I critiqued him.

00:49:55.666 --> 00:49:57.540
I'll show you when I filmed it.

00:49:57.540 --> 00:49:58.940
Even when he films the fireworks,

00:49:58.940 --> 00:49:59.953
he's filming just below the fireworks.

00:49:59.953 --> 00:50:00.863
Look at that.

00:50:02.460 --> 00:50:04.170
Is the pier catching on fire?

00:50:04.170 --> 00:50:05.460
Well we'll get to that in a second.

00:50:05.460 --> 00:50:09.450
Oh, what?

00:50:09.450 --> 00:50:11.260
I'll show you one that I filmed.

00:50:11.260 --> 00:50:16.210
This is, now, you can see
a flame down there, right?

00:50:16.210 --> 00:50:17.610
You see that flame?
Yeah.

00:50:18.620 --> 00:50:20.430
What is that flame?
Well, it's one

00:50:20.430 --> 00:50:22.239
of the things, I didn't
put this on Instagram

00:50:22.239 --> 00:50:24.003
'cause I was a little
embarrassed about this.

00:50:24.870 --> 00:50:26.190
The zooming is--

00:50:26.190 --> 00:50:27.730
That's Locke.
A bit nauseating.

00:50:27.730 --> 00:50:28.730
Now when I filmed,

00:50:30.274 --> 00:50:31.940
ran back.

00:50:31.940 --> 00:50:36.637
Guys, a bunch of rednecks out
there with their shirts off.

00:50:36.637 --> 00:50:38.460
My goodness.
Now I'll show you.

00:50:38.460 --> 00:50:39.903
That's one set?

00:50:41.058 --> 00:50:41.975
Oh my gosh.

00:50:44.820 --> 00:50:46.270
These are huge.
That's huge.

00:50:46.270 --> 00:50:48.210
These are massive, you
never seen this before.

00:50:48.210 --> 00:50:49.562
You never been present for one of these?

00:50:49.562 --> 00:50:50.395
Uh-uh.

00:50:50.395 --> 00:50:52.490
I mean like it's a legit firework show.

00:50:52.490 --> 00:50:55.230
You thought I was talking
like little bottle rockets?

00:50:55.230 --> 00:50:56.630
I didn't know it was that big.

00:50:56.630 --> 00:51:00.063
No no, and listen,
you're right next to it.

00:51:00.980 --> 00:51:03.430
In fact, we had--
You didn't spray?

00:51:03.430 --> 00:51:05.140
We had five leftover and we took 'em

00:51:05.140 --> 00:51:06.670
to the other beach house which is up

00:51:06.670 --> 00:51:10.150
on the Intracoastal Waterway
like up on the Neuse River.

00:51:10.150 --> 00:51:11.880
Okay.
And there's another dock.

00:51:11.880 --> 00:51:14.490
We take it out on that and
I lit like six that night.

00:51:14.490 --> 00:51:18.730
This is Saturday night
so July 7th I guess.

00:51:18.730 --> 00:51:21.970
Just a couple nights ago
and technically I think

00:51:21.970 --> 00:51:23.400
they're illegal by that point.

00:51:23.400 --> 00:51:24.690
Oh yeah they're not turning a blind eye.

00:51:24.690 --> 00:51:25.720
But we had more.

00:51:25.720 --> 00:51:29.512
You're going out on a dock so as to--

00:51:29.512 --> 00:51:31.360
For the view.

00:51:31.360 --> 00:51:33.460
Oh you're not doing that
so if it catches on fire,

00:51:33.460 --> 00:51:35.880
the whole will just burn into
the water and then it's safe.

00:51:35.880 --> 00:51:37.600
Well that does help.

00:51:37.600 --> 00:51:39.000
Because I'll get back
to how we had to handle

00:51:39.000 --> 00:51:40.133
that fire in a second.

00:51:42.350 --> 00:51:44.420
There was one where the
wind was coming back

00:51:44.420 --> 00:51:47.170
towards us and they
don't always go as high

00:51:47.170 --> 00:51:50.610
as they should and
there's one that blew up

00:51:50.610 --> 00:51:52.540
and then it blows up into a
bunch of different pieces,

00:51:52.540 --> 00:51:55.250
then it blows up again
in smaller explosions.

00:51:55.250 --> 00:51:57.220
I swear there was one that happened where

00:51:57.220 --> 00:51:59.420
the second small explosion,
I'm the one closest

00:51:59.420 --> 00:52:00.430
to the action 'cause I light it,

00:52:00.430 --> 00:52:02.360
then I kinda go down the stairs,

00:52:02.360 --> 00:52:03.860
'cause this dock had like two levels

00:52:03.860 --> 00:52:05.130
and then I'm kinda watching it.

00:52:05.130 --> 00:52:07.630
One came, I swear that I was getting hit

00:52:07.630 --> 00:52:09.130
with the little sparks.

00:52:09.130 --> 00:52:10.630
It's not safe.

00:52:10.630 --> 00:52:13.080
As I said on the Instagram, I gave a,

00:52:13.080 --> 00:52:14.750
my wife made me do a disclaimer.

00:52:14.750 --> 00:52:16.760
Jessie was like you
need to do a disclaimer

00:52:16.760 --> 00:52:18.340
so people don't do this and I said,

00:52:18.340 --> 00:52:20.370
my wife wants me to give you a disclaimer.

00:52:20.370 --> 00:52:22.824
Do not do this unless
you're a redneck is what--

00:52:24.055 --> 00:52:24.888
Is what I said.

00:52:24.888 --> 00:52:28.450
As cool as this looks and
as much fun as I'm having--

00:52:28.450 --> 00:52:30.663
You will hurt yourself
if you're not a redneck.

00:52:31.770 --> 00:52:34.170
But so, fire time,

00:52:34.170 --> 00:52:36.967
so Jessie first of all she says,

00:52:36.967 --> 00:52:38.610
"Do you have a fire extinguisher?"

00:52:38.610 --> 00:52:40.090
Right before we're
getting ready to do this

00:52:40.090 --> 00:52:42.609
and I'm like, "No."

00:52:42.609 --> 00:52:45.521
And I was like we're
doing it over the water.

00:52:45.521 --> 00:52:47.560
She's like you need to
have a fire extinguisher

00:52:47.560 --> 00:52:50.330
and I was like okay so
they had one at this house

00:52:50.330 --> 00:52:53.180
like underneath the sink and what happens

00:52:53.180 --> 00:52:55.440
after these things go off as you can see,

00:52:55.440 --> 00:52:59.992
it's so hot that sometimes
they just end up catching fire

00:52:59.992 --> 00:53:03.830
after all the stuff's gone and so,

00:53:03.830 --> 00:53:05.760
I'm looking at that and
me and Chris were trying

00:53:05.760 --> 00:53:07.320
to figure out what to do so we ran

00:53:07.320 --> 00:53:11.310
and got the fire extinguisher
but they basically expended,

00:53:11.310 --> 00:53:13.090
it was one of the fire
extinguishers like we used

00:53:13.090 --> 00:53:15.890
in the T-Shirt War
where it was the powder.

00:53:15.890 --> 00:53:16.870
Oh.
And it runs out

00:53:16.870 --> 00:53:19.610
real fast and then we
had more fires happening

00:53:19.610 --> 00:53:22.920
so then we had to send
the boys, the fire brigade

00:53:22.920 --> 00:53:25.390
to the house to get buckets
and we took sound water

00:53:25.390 --> 00:53:27.210
and started throwing it
all over the fireworks

00:53:27.210 --> 00:53:30.120
and I will say that
one of them was on fire

00:53:30.120 --> 00:53:32.050
before the water got
there and had to throw it

00:53:32.050 --> 00:53:35.483
into the water but we did
get Isaiah to go retrieve it.

00:53:36.376 --> 00:53:38.270
He had to go to the next dock.

00:53:38.270 --> 00:53:39.470
The damp--

00:53:39.470 --> 00:53:41.200
Yeah.
Trash.

00:53:41.200 --> 00:53:44.090
Yeah so everything's good.

00:53:44.090 --> 00:53:45.540
We're still good.

00:53:45.540 --> 00:53:47.450
We still good?
Yeah we're still good.

00:53:47.450 --> 00:53:49.500
Man, you make me nervous.

00:53:49.500 --> 00:53:51.730
Now and--
That's why I'm not there.

00:53:51.730 --> 00:53:52.700
I understand that--
We can't both be there.

00:53:52.700 --> 00:53:54.940
I do understand that it's not responsible.

00:53:54.940 --> 00:53:56.710
Somebody's gotta carry on.
It's not responsible.

00:53:56.710 --> 00:53:58.860
One of these rednecks has gotta carry on.

00:53:59.930 --> 00:54:02.220
But you know what, you
gotta have a little fun

00:54:02.220 --> 00:54:04.520
every once in awhile
man, you gotta celebrate.

00:54:05.450 --> 00:54:07.840
But it was so hot.

00:54:07.840 --> 00:54:11.173
I forget how hot it is.

00:54:11.173 --> 00:54:13.518
We'll find out next week.

00:54:13.518 --> 00:54:15.220
♪ Hotter than the Fourth of July ♪

00:54:15.220 --> 00:54:16.780
We'll be back in North
Carolina for something

00:54:16.780 --> 00:54:19.665
that we'll tell you about
that's novel-related.

00:54:19.665 --> 00:54:21.820
But we're going out there next week.

00:54:21.820 --> 00:54:23.970
That's right.
It's unbelievably hot.

00:54:23.970 --> 00:54:28.090
I walked outside, my frickin'
sunglasses fogged up.

00:54:28.090 --> 00:54:30.730
I actually don't remember
that happening growing up

00:54:30.730 --> 00:54:32.150
in North Carolina, I walk outside,

00:54:32.150 --> 00:54:34.510
they just fogged up
completely 'cause I was going

00:54:34.510 --> 00:54:36.140
from AC but--

00:54:36.140 --> 00:54:39.430
Your face, your California
face gets hotter.

00:54:39.430 --> 00:54:41.170
Has adjusted?
No.

00:54:41.170 --> 00:54:44.360
It's not adjusted so when you
go back to North Carolina,

00:54:44.360 --> 00:54:48.380
your California face just,
it's like oh my gosh,

00:54:48.380 --> 00:54:50.530
my face is so hot, I gotta get rid of it

00:54:50.530 --> 00:54:53.280
and it pushes it onto the
glasses and they fog up.

00:54:53.280 --> 00:54:55.190
Oh that's very scientific.
But if you live there

00:54:55.190 --> 00:54:57.630
all the time, it's like, you learn how

00:54:57.630 --> 00:54:59.050
to not fog up your glasses.

00:54:59.050 --> 00:55:00.160
And it rained--

00:55:00.160 --> 00:55:01.380
Your face learns that.

00:55:01.380 --> 00:55:03.377
It rained every day.
That didn't help.

00:55:03.377 --> 00:55:06.830
But you didn't care because
you were already wet.

00:55:06.830 --> 00:55:10.580
You cannot just, you just
go outside and you're wet.

00:55:10.580 --> 00:55:13.510
You immediately start
sweating from everywhere.

00:55:13.510 --> 00:55:16.230
That's why you said a bunch of rednecks

00:55:16.230 --> 00:55:19.900
not having shirts on but
you very quickly learn,

00:55:19.900 --> 00:55:21.300
I might as well not have a shirt on.

00:55:21.300 --> 00:55:23.080
We might as well be naked.

00:55:23.080 --> 00:55:24.670
Because any clothes that you have

00:55:24.670 --> 00:55:25.900
just immediately stick to you.

00:55:25.900 --> 00:55:28.150
I put jeans on to get back on the plane

00:55:28.150 --> 00:55:30.890
coming back yesterday
and I just had jeans on

00:55:30.890 --> 00:55:34.230
to basically walk to
my car and I was like,

00:55:34.230 --> 00:55:37.110
I remember now why we always wore shorts.

00:55:37.110 --> 00:55:41.050
Like me and you would
go to work in shorts.

00:55:41.050 --> 00:55:42.960
I remember there was a point.

00:55:42.960 --> 00:55:44.650
I actually remember--
Shorts and flip flops.

00:55:44.650 --> 00:55:48.110
I remember when we were
filming the documentary

00:55:48.110 --> 00:55:50.610
Looking for Ms. Locklear,
we had a conversation.

00:55:50.610 --> 00:55:52.020
We wore jeans 'cause we
didn't wanna look like

00:55:52.020 --> 00:55:53.320
a couple of nimrods--
Dumbasses.

00:55:53.320 --> 00:55:54.840
Walking around in shorts.
Right.

00:55:54.840 --> 00:55:56.653
But we were so hot.
= We were so hot.

00:55:56.653 --> 00:55:57.940
The whole summer.
That was the first time

00:55:57.940 --> 00:56:00.460
we made a conscious
choice to only wear pants.

00:56:00.460 --> 00:56:03.192
Did you feel like a dumbass
wearing shorts everywhere?

00:56:03.192 --> 00:56:05.360
Wearing shorts, a little
bit but I adjusted.

00:56:05.360 --> 00:56:06.580
I adjusted quickly, man.

00:56:06.580 --> 00:56:09.772
Yeah it's not, you shouldn't
feel that way but we do.

00:56:09.772 --> 00:56:10.690
No no.

00:56:10.690 --> 00:56:12.930
Something about shorts
just makes you seem like--

00:56:12.930 --> 00:56:13.763
And I do not--

00:56:13.763 --> 00:56:16.250
What does it make you seem like?

00:56:16.250 --> 00:56:18.334
A bare-legged man.

00:56:18.334 --> 00:56:19.565
I don't know.

00:56:19.565 --> 00:56:21.850
If they're pleated and
you can tuck your shirt

00:56:21.850 --> 00:56:23.460
into it then you're back.

00:56:23.460 --> 00:56:25.130
That's not the kind that I had.

00:56:25.130 --> 00:56:27.910
You're total, you're just the dad.

00:56:27.910 --> 00:56:30.660
It gets so hot, it gets super
hot in California as well.

00:56:30.660 --> 00:56:34.040
It'll be like 110 towards
the end of the summer here.

00:56:34.040 --> 00:56:36.770
But it's never so hot that
you actually feel like

00:56:36.770 --> 00:56:38.323
I gotta get into shorts.

00:56:39.600 --> 00:56:41.020
'Cause the humidity's so different.

00:56:41.020 --> 00:56:44.120
Do you feel like there is a way for you

00:56:44.120 --> 00:56:46.960
to feel cool and I don't
mean temperature-wise,

00:56:46.960 --> 00:56:49.520
I mean like trendy.

00:56:49.520 --> 00:56:51.254
In shorts?
In shorts.

00:56:51.254 --> 00:56:53.363
I don't see an option.

00:56:54.913 --> 00:56:57.520
I think for us--
That's a good question.

00:56:57.520 --> 00:57:00.800
I just don't think we can pull off shorts.

00:57:00.800 --> 00:57:02.677
I think there are shorts out
there that are trendy shorts.

00:57:02.677 --> 00:57:04.460
I just don't have them.

00:57:04.460 --> 00:57:07.592
And I don't know what
constitutes a trendy short.

00:57:07.592 --> 00:57:10.440
I think they're a little bit
shorter than those shorts

00:57:10.440 --> 00:57:12.253
that I wear probably.

00:57:13.090 --> 00:57:15.020
Got more interesting patterns.

00:57:15.020 --> 00:57:16.490
A little shorter.

00:57:16.490 --> 00:57:17.797
I think you gotta have like a tank top on

00:57:17.797 --> 00:57:20.060
and maybe a visor.

00:57:20.060 --> 00:57:23.610
None of those things can you pull off.

00:57:23.610 --> 00:57:24.490
I can't either.

00:57:24.490 --> 00:57:26.070
Yeah well we should try.

00:57:26.070 --> 00:57:28.370
I think I can pull off shorts.

00:57:28.370 --> 00:57:29.900
Well I think I can pull off shorts.

00:57:29.900 --> 00:57:30.910
I don't think you can.

00:57:30.910 --> 00:57:32.370
You see my legs?

00:57:32.370 --> 00:57:35.860
I'm not commementing,
commementing on your legs.

00:57:35.860 --> 00:57:39.250
I'm just saying just the
exposed nature of your legs.

00:57:39.250 --> 00:57:40.740
I have a lot of leg.

00:57:40.740 --> 00:57:42.813
I mean your thigh's at
eye-level to some people.

00:57:42.813 --> 00:57:44.463
Yeah I have a lot of leg, I do.

00:57:45.537 --> 00:57:47.965
Couple other notable things.

00:57:47.965 --> 00:57:51.100
I drove around a car
that was an advertisement

00:57:51.100 --> 00:57:52.750
